---
name: review-task
version: 3.1.0
description: lead phase-ending code review assignment for atm-bd-orchestration. task_id is a review bead the lead creates under the phase feature. Read-only; the lead files the findings as finding beads.
format: xml
required_variables:
  - task_id
  - phase
  - branch
  - commit
  - worktree_path
  - review_focus
  - review_questions
  - references
  - lead
  - cc
  - integration_branch
metadata:
  type: review-task
  tags:
    - stage:review
    - orchestration:beads
  workflow:
    scope:
      kind: phase
      variable: phase
    state: review-start
    stage: review
    transition: start
---
<atm-review id="{{ task_id }}" phase="{{ phase }}">
  <description><![CDATA[Phase-ending code review and finding post-mortem on the integrated phase branch. Read-only analysis only; no code changes.]]></description>

  <branch><![CDATA[{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}]]></branch>
  <commit><![CDATA[{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}]]></commit>
  <worktree><![CDATA[{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}]]></worktree>

  <review-focus><![CDATA[
{{ review_focus | string | cdata_escape }}
  ]]></review-focus>

  <review-questions><![CDATA[
{{ review_questions | string | cdata_escape }}
  ]]></review-questions>

  <references><![CDATA[
{{ references | string | cdata_escape }}
  ]]></references>

  <output-format><![CDATA[
    Structured findings report with sections:
    1. SUMMARY — one-paragraph verdict
    2. FINDINGS — numbered list, each with severity (blocking, important, minor), location, finding, recommendation
    3. EXTRACTION-READINESS — score 1-5 per relevant module with rationale
    4. RECOMMENDED-NEXT-SPRINTS — concrete follow-up work items
    5. INTEGRATION POST-MORTEM — follow .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/references/post-mortem.md: all finding ids and dispositions, evidence at the assigned integration SHA, inventory totals, integration_review_passed or integration_review_failed, and useful systemic follow-ups (or none).
  ]]></output-format>

  <workflow>
    <step id="a"><![CDATA[Check readiness, claim the bead, then start the task. If it is not ready, report its blockers to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} and wait.]]></step>
    <step id="b"><![CDATA[Require branch `{{ integration_branch | string | cdata_escape }}` (the phase root's `metadata.integration_branch`); a stack-tip review cannot substitute for integration verification. Verify that `{{ worktree_path | string | cdata_escape }}` is on `{{ branch | string | cdata_escape }}` with `HEAD` exactly `{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}`; reject a mismatched or moving checkout. Read relevant source and doc files at the assigned commit with `git show {{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}:<path>`. The phase plan is `bd show <phase feature>` and its children (`bd list --parent <phase feature> --all -n 0`).]]></step>
    <step id="b1"><![CDATA[Read .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/references/post-mortem.md and reconcile every phase finding, including closed and nested findings, at the assigned commit. Run the JEV workflow in references/post-mortem-context-preparation.md, then investigate every unsure/flagged result. Populate `integration_review`, `post_mortem_md`, and `post_mortem_jev` (status, run_ids, jsonl_path, integration_sha, reason) in the completion variables. JEV unavailability or unchecked findings leaves integration review pending. Missing or unverified resolutions prevent an integration PASS.]]></step>
    <step id="c"><![CDATA[Do not make any code changes and do not write to beads; {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} files each finding as a finding bead from your report.]]></step>
    <step id="d"><![CDATA[Re-verify every cited file:line at `{{ commit | string | cdata_escape }}`, never at a newer branch tip or moving worktree state; stale or missing evidence is a finding, not a guess. Before closing, run `python3 .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/scripts/check-review-completion.py <scratch>/review-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`; require exit 0. This checks counts, verdict and the pinned SHA, not the truth of source evidence. Then close the bead and the task together: `bd close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --reason "review complete: <verdict>"` and `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} completed --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/review-complete.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/review-complete-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`, with the vars built from that template's `required_variables` (`task_id`, `phase` and `commit` unchanged) and written outside the repository. Only this close frees your queue.{% if cc and cc != lead %} Copy a one-line summary to `{{ cc | string | cdata_escape }}` with a plain `atm send {{ cc | string | cdata_escape }} --stdin`.{% endif %}]]></step>
    <step id="d1"><![CDATA[If the review cannot be performed, reuse an existing workflow class bead for the same failure signature: append the task id, head, command and failure evidence, and cite the class id in the refusal. If no class matches, report the signature to {{ lead | string | cdata_escape }} for classification and cite that message instead; do not create a per-task shadow or delay the refusal. Return the bead with `bd update {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} --status open --assignee "" --append-notes "REVIEW_CANNOT_RUN; workflow class or escalation <reference>: <reason>"`, and close the task with `atm task close {{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }} refused --template .claude/skills/atm-bd-orchestration/templates/task-refused.md.j2 --vars <scratch>/refused-{{ task_id | string | cdata_escape }}-vars.json`.]]></step>
  </workflow>
</atm-review>
